From 843793732e8ee7de1d0af763ecd942246918cf49 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: l4h <z@univalence.me>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 01:42:58 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] mcs: do not enqueue a remote thread whose new budget is not
 ready

SchedControl_ConfigureFlags on a scheduling context whose thread is
runnable on another core calls schedContext_resume(), which postpones
the thread to the release queue when its (updated) refills are not
ready yet, and then enqueued it in the remote core's ready queue
anyway. Debug kernels fail the isSchedulable() assertion in
tcbSchedEnqueue(); release kernels put the thread in both queues.

This happens whenever a budget-limited SC is reconfigured on its own
core from another core while its budget is used up, for example when a
CPU cap is changed or a capped vCPU's budget is redistributed.

Enqueue only a schedulable thread, as possibleSwitchTo() does locally.
A postponed one still needs remoteQueueUpdate(): the remote core may be
idle and must reprogram its timer for the release.
---
 src/object/schedcontrol.c | 6 +++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/object/schedcontrol.c b/src/object/schedcontrol.c
index c3ee1498e..a99cba828 100644
--- a/src/object/schedcontrol.c
+++ b/src/object/schedcontrol.c
@@ -63,8 +63,12 @@ static exception_t invokeSchedControl_ConfigureFlags(sched_context_t *target, wo
             if (isRunnable(thread) && thread != NODE_STATE(ksCurThread)) {
                 possibleSwitchTo(thread);
             }
-        } else if (isRunnable(thread)) {
+        } else if (isSchedulable(thread)) {
             SCHED_ENQUEUE(thread);
+        } else if (isRunnable(thread)) {
+            /* schedContext_resume() put it in the release queue: the core
+             * must still reprogram its timer for the release. */
+            SMP_COND_STATEMENT(remoteQueueUpdate(thread));
         }
         if (thread == NODE_STATE(ksCurThread)) {
             rescheduleRequired();
-- 
